From: Bellatrix contributors
Subject: [PATCH] WirelessManager: wake the event loop from the GUI process

Pressing Scan in the WirelessManager window did nothing: no scan was
requested, no S2_GETNETWORKS reached the SANA-II driver, and no error was
reported anywhere.

The GUI runs in its own process (main_amiga.c start_gui() spawns
"WirelessManager GUI") while wpa_supplicant_run() -> eloop_run() blocks in
the main task. ScanFunc() calls wpa_supplicant_req_scan(), which registers an
eloop timeout from the GUI process -- and nothing wakes the task that would
run it.

eloop_run() builds its signal mask once per iteration, before Wait(), from
the timeouts and read sockets that exist at that moment. With no networks
configured there is neither: the mask is zero, and Wait(0) can never return,
because no signal satisfies an empty mask. The loop is parked for ever and
the timeout the GUI just queued is never seen. Even with sockets registered
the mask is a snapshot, so a timeout added afterwards waits for an unrelated
wakeup.

Give the loop a signal of its own, always in the mask, and signal it from
eloop_register_timeout() when the caller is another task. Re-arm the timer
request when the newly registered timeout is nearer than the one already in
flight, which is the case that matters here -- the Scan button asks for a
scan at zero seconds. The list splice now runs under Forbid(), since it has
two writers.

Note eloop.wake_sig cannot rely on the memset: 0 is a valid signal number, so
it is assigned for real and set to -1 when none is free (in which case the
old behaviour returns rather than a wrong signal being used).

---
--- a/workbench/network/WirelessManager/src/utils/eloop_amiga.c
+++ b/workbench/network/WirelessManager/src/utils/eloop_amiga.c
@@ -68,10 +68,25 @@
 
 	struct MsgPort *timer_port;
 	struct timerequest *timer_request;
+
+	/*
+	 * The task running eloop_run(), and a signal for waking it.
+	 *
+	 * Timeouts are registered from other tasks: the MUI GUI runs in its own
+	 * process ("WirelessManager GUI"), and its Scan button calls
+	 * wpa_supplicant_req_scan() there. Without a way to wake this task, that
+	 * registration is invisible until something else happens to wake it --
+	 * and with no networks configured and no read sockets, nothing ever
+	 * does, because sig_mask below is then zero and Wait(0) never returns.
+	 */
+	struct Task *task;
+	BYTE wake_sig;
 };
 
 static struct eloop_data eloop;
 
+static void eloop_wake(void);
+
 
